.\" Manual page for the mosdns-router DNS router as this package installs it.
.TH MOSDNS\-ROUTER 8 "2026-09-27" "mosdns-router 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mosdns\-router \- the machine's DNS router, on loopback only
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mosdns\-router
.B start
.B \-c
.I /etc/mosdns/mosdns.yaml
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR mosdns\-router (8)
is the resolver this package makes a machine's own. It binds
.B 127.0.0.1:53
on UDP and TCP, and nothing else on any other address; it also opens a second
loopback listener, on
.B 127.0.0.1:15354
with no address outside the loopback, which is where
.BR "mosdns\-cdnctl" (1)
asks it to drop the foreign cache. It is started by
.BR mosdns\-router.service (5).
The routing document it reads is
.IR /etc/mosdns/mosdns.yaml ,
and that document is GENERATED: it is the output of
.BR "mosdns\-cdnctl render" (1)
for the policy in
.IR /etc/mosdns/policy.yaml .
A hand edit to it is an edit the next render overwrites, and the file says in its
own header that it is generated and by what.
.SS The two branches
There are two and there is no third. A name the pinned China list matches goes to
the DNS servers NetworkManager has in use for that device, and every other name
goes to the loopback DNSCrypt resolver over TCP. There is no fallback between them:
a foreign query that cannot be answered fails, and is never re\-asked inside the
network the user is leaving.
.SS "Where the domestic branch gets its resolvers"
A NetworkManager dispatcher hook publishes the resolvers in use, and the router
reads what it published. The hook is not this package's idea of a DHCP client: it
asks NetworkManager, in order, for the raw lease's
.B domain_name_servers
, then this event's own dispatcher variables, then the device's effective DNS, and
only then
.BR "resolvectl dns" .
The first of those that answers is the one recorded, and it is recorded as the
SOURCE rather than the event \(em a lease renewal, an interface coming up, and a
resolver list that changed are one state when the addresses did not move, and
treating them as three would flush the router's cache for resolvers that never
changed.
.PP
So a
.B manually configured
resolver is found, and found the ordinary way: a connection with
.B ipv4.method manual
has no lease at all, the lease queries answer with nothing, and the device's
effective DNS is the operator's own
.BR ipv4.dns .
The event that carries it is
.BR dns\-change ,
which is the one whose name promises a lease and never delivers one.
.PP
Two things about that case are worth knowing.
.IP \(bu 2
The install records and restores
.BR ipv4.dns ,
.BR ipv6.dns ,
and both
.B ignore\-auto\-dns
properties, so an operator's manual resolver list survives an uninstall. It is
recorded whether or not this package changed it, because a hand restore is how a
machine ends up with no DNS.
.IP \(bu 2
A statically configured resolver that is
.B changed
is picked up without rebooting the interface. The hook fires on
.B dns\-change
and the router adopts the new list on its next query.
.PP
The one thing that is not automatic is the resolver being reachable. This package
records what the machine was told to use; it does not keep a hand\-typed address
alive.
.SH WHEN THE NETWORK CHANGES
The hook runs for
.BR up ,
.BR dhcp4\-change ,
.BR dhcp6\-change " and " dns\-change ,
and does nothing at all for any other event NetworkManager sends it \(em it exits
zero without touching a file, because NetworkManager calls it for events that have
nothing to do with DNS and an error there would be noise in its log).
.PP
.B down
is the one event that publishes rather than collects, and it publishes an
EMPTY list: the addresses the collector would find belong to a lease that no
longer exists. The router's domestic branch then fails closed, by design \(em
forwarding a domestic query to a resolver on a network that is gone would be a
worse answer than no answer. Foreign names are unaffected; they go to the loopback
DNSCrypt resolver and never depended on the lease.
.PP
On a machine that is unplugged for a while, that means domestic names do not
resolve until the interface comes back and a
.B up
or
.B dhcp4\-change
event arrives. This is the shipped behaviour and it is a policy rather than a
default:
.B dhcp.failure_policy
in
.IR /etc/mosdns/policy.yaml
is
.BR disable\-current ,
and
.B use\-last\-good
instead keeps serving the last published set that had resolvers and reports how old
it is. Both are honest answers; only one of them will talk to a dead address.
.SS "What the router does while nothing has changed"
It re\-reads the published state on every query rather than at start\-up, comparing
the file's device and inode as well as its size and timestamp, so a replacement
that happens to be the same length and the same age is still a new document. A
generation that is not newer than the one in service is refused, and a read that
fails leaves the current generation serving and is retried on the next query rather
than at the next event.
.PP
A replaced generation keeps its clients for sixty seconds while the new one takes
over, so a resolver change does not interrupt an exchange already in flight. That
window is a fixed property of the plugin and not a policy key: the only settings
for
.B dhcp_forward
are in the generated routing document, and
.IR dhcp.failure_policy
in
.I /etc/mosdns/policy.yaml
is the one an operator is expected to change.
.PP
To see what the router believes, without reading JSON:
.RB " sudo mosdns\-cdnctl status \-\-dhcp /run/mosdns/dhcp\-upstreams.json"
.SS What is rewritten
Only what comes back from the foreign branch, and only that:
.B cdn_rewrite
runs before the cache, so a rewritten answer is never itself stored, and a cached
answer is rewritten on the way out to every client rather than stored rewritten.
It replaces an address only for a response every one of whose addresses is inside
a published range, and only while the published selection's proof window is open.
Nothing in the domestic branch is rewritten: those answers come from the network
the user is not leaving, and nothing here has proved anything about them.
.SH THE RESOLVER WATCHDOG
.BR mosdns\-watchdog.service (5)
is the one unit in this package that changes the machine with nobody watching.
It runs every minute, asks this machine's own resolver one question, and when the
answer is no for long enough it runs
.BR "mosdns\-cdnctl emergency\-rollback" (1)
**by itself**.

This is a default and it is switchable, and both facts matter. The
.B automatic
key in
.I /etc/mosdns/watchdog.yaml
is
.B true
as shipped.
.SS What it restores, and what it does not
The rollback puts the DNS settings the install RECORDED back on the connection
\- on a machine this package has taken over, the resolvers the machine's own DHCP
lease published \- so ordinary name resolution works again.

**Foreign\-name resolution through DNSCrypt does not work afterwards.** That is
the condition this package was installed to fix, and the rollback does not fix
it: it stops nothing, removes nothing and starts nothing, so the router, the
resolver and the configuration are all still installed and the local resolver is
still not serving. The fault is no longer in the way; it is not fixed. The
watchdog says both halves in its own message every time it acts, and the message
is in the journal where the action is.
.SS Why it waits
A single failed probe is not evidence that the router is down: a slow upstream, a
cold boot before NTP has synchronised and a loaded machine each produce one.
With automatic rollback as the default, acting on one would be a mechanism that
can tear down a working configuration while somebody is using it, and a
self\-inflicted outage is worse than a false alarm. So the default acts on the
.BR 3rd
consecutive failure, or once
.B 10
minutes have passed since the first, whichever comes first \- and both numbers
are in
.IR /etc/mosdns/watchdog.yaml ,
because the false\-positive rate has never been measured and a number nobody
measured does not get baked in as a constant.
.PP
The ten minutes are measured on the machine's **boot clock**, not on the wall
clock. A machine whose clock steps \- a cold boot before NTP has synchronised, a
hypervisor adjusting it, a person running
.B hwclock
\- must not be able to satisfy the window by having had its clock moved, and a
cold boot with a wrong clock is exactly the case the first sentence of this
section names. The boot clock cannot be stepped and it keeps counting across a
suspend, which is the case the elapsed half exists for.
.PP
An **empty** setting file is a refusal, and an **absent** one is not. A file
that is not there is a deletion, and a deletion is not a decision, so the
shipped default stands; a file that is there and says nothing is a statement
this program will not act on, because a truncated file, a
.B "sed \-i"
that matched every line and a deployment that wrote a placeholder all leave one.
.SS While something else is changing the machine
An install transaction restarts the two units that serve
.BR 127.0.0.1:53 ,
so during an upgrade the local resolver is not answering by construction \- the
transaction creates that failure deliberately. The watchdog therefore takes the
shared control lock before it counts or acts, and while anything else holds that
lock it counts nothing, acts on nothing, and says which lock and who holds it.
The transaction holds the same lock for the window in which it moves the
machine's DNS, and for its rollback. The first run after a boot is also three
minutes after boot, for the same underlying reason.
.SS Turning it off
Set
.B automatic: false
in
.IR /etc/mosdns/watchdog.yaml .
The probe keeps running and the messages keep appearing; what stops is the
unattended part.
.BR "mosdns\-cdnctl emergency\-rollback" (1)
is unaffected, because a switch that stopped the action for the operator as well
would leave a broken machine with no way back. Turning the mechanism back on
starts its window again rather than resuming a streak that was interrupted.
.SS If the setting cannot be read
The watchdog does nothing and says so loudly, because a setting it cannot read
exactly is one it would have to guess at \- and guessing there decides whether a
working machine is taken apart. That is deliberately the opposite of this
package's usual fail\-open. The message says the machine is not being watched.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mosdns/watchdog.yaml
The watchdog's setting: whether automatic rollback is on, the consecutive\-failure
count, and the elapsed\-time window. Ships with
.B automatic: true
and the two numbers the unit's header explains; a conffile, so an upgrade asks
before replacing an operator's
.BR automatic:\ false .
Its own comments say what a rollback restores and what it does not, because an
operator who has read only this file has to be able to learn that.
.TP
.I /etc/mosdns/mosdns.yaml
The routing document. Generated; a conffile, so an upgrade asks before replacing
it. Regenerate it with
.BR "mosdns\-cdnctl render" (1).
.TP
.I /etc/mosdns/policy.yaml
The policy both generated documents are rendered from. Edit this, not the
documents. Validate it with
.BR "mosdns\-cdnctl validate" (1).
.PP
Its
.B foreign
block names the route foreign queries leave by:
.B foreign.upstreams
is a list whose entries are either the packaged resolver
.RB ( kind:\ dnscrypt ,
which carries no address because that listener is the resolver's own) or an
address mosdns dials
.RB ( kind:\ upstream ).
.B foreign.concurrent
is how many of them one query is sent to, and
.B 1
is a random pick rather than a race \(em the shipped value is
.BR 2 .
Each upstream's
.B bootstrap
resolves that upstream's own host name and is
.I not
a query path.
.B foreign_cache
carries the router's own answer cache size and, optionally, a record-time bound
in seconds.
.PP
A policy left with no enabled entry the ECH key can be fetched through is refused
by name rather than rendered, because the consequence of rendering it is that every
force\-ECH domain fails closed once the key's grace ends with nothing in the log
saying why. Add a
.B dnscrypt
entry, or one whose
.B addr
is
.BR tcp:// .
.PP
.BR "mosdns\-cdnctl check\-upstream" (1)
reports which of them can be reached, and
.BR "mosdns\-cdnctl flush\-cache" (1)
empties the router's answer cache.
.TP
.I /etc/mosdns/force\-ech\-domains.txt
Domains to force ECH for. One per line; a line whose first non\-space character is
# is a comment, a blank line is nothing, and everything after a # on a line ends the
entry. Ships with no entries, and carries a comment line rather than being zero bytes
because a zero\-byte list is refused.
.PP
No restart is needed to edit this file: the rewriter reads it about twice a second,
so a new entry is in force immediately. /etc/mosdns/policy.yaml is the file that
needs
.BR "systemctl restart mosdns\-router" ,
because
.BR ech.enabled ,
.BR ech.failure_policy
and
.BR ech.stale_grace
are resolved once at start\-up. This file is a conffile, so a package upgrade asks
before replacing it.
.PP
Forcing a name is not free. The router answers the name's HTTPS (type 65) query
itself, and the ECH key it installs is BORROWED from the sources named in the policy
rather than published by the origin for that name. A listed name's A and AAAA queries
are ordinary queries and are answered as such: this router runs only on the foreign
path, so the HTTPS query has already disclosed the name to the foreign resolver
before any address lookup follows, and withholding the address bought no privacy
while leaving the client with nothing to connect to. The synthesized record carries
the address the CDN selector proved, or the upstream's own published address when
there is no proved one, and no hint at all when there is neither.
.PP
A browser must be told to use the key or it will arrive and be ignored. Firefox 129
and later default both of these to the unsafe side, and both are in about:config:
.B network.dns.force_waiting_https_rr
must be true, or a slow HTTPS record is treated as no HTTPS record and the browser
silently connects unencrypted; and
.B network.dns.echconfig.fallback_to_origin_when_all_failed
must be false, or a failed ECH connection falls back to the plaintext origin name.
This package does not set them and does not read your browser profile, so this is
stated, not checked.
.TP
.I /etc/mosdns/cloudflare.txt
Addresses and prefixes to measure in addition to the published ones. Ships empty.
.TP
.I /etc/mosdns/cloudfront\-domains.yaml
Per\-hostname CloudFront candidate profiles. Ships with an empty profile list.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mosdns/runtime/ech\-state.json
The ECH state the response rewriter publishes. Generated; the router's only write.
.TP
.I /run/mosdns/dhcp\-upstreams.json
The DHCP generation the router follows, published by the NetworkManager dispatcher
hook. Its absence is not an error: the router keeps the generation it has and
starts green.
.SH STATE DIRECTORIES
The state directories are
.BR /var/lib/mosdns ,
.BR /var/lib/mosdns/runtime ,
.B /var/lib/mosdns/lists
and
.BR /run/mosdns ,
each
.BR root:mosdns\-router " 2770"
with a default ACL granting the group
.BR rwx ,
and it is the one exception that matters:
.B /run/mosdns
is
.B 2750
with no ACL, because only
.BR root
writes it \(em the NetworkManager dispatcher that runs the DHCP bridge is a
dispatcher script, so it runs as root \(em and a group
.B w
on that directory would let any member of
.BR mosdns\-router
unlink and replace
.IR dhcp-upstreams.json ,
since unlink and rename are decided by the directory rather than by the mode of
the file inside it. One group serves two service users under
.IR /var/lib/mosdns ,
because a default ACL grants by group and per\-service groups would let only one of
them replace the other's files.
.B /var/lib/mosdns
is provisioned by the package's
.B postinst
and
.B /run/mosdns
by the package's
.BR tmpfiles.d (5)
entry, because
.B /run
is a tmpfs and the group, the setgid bit and the default ACL do not survive a
reboot.
.PP
One further directory is the exception and is not in that set:
.BR /run/mosdns/watchdog ,
which is
.BR root:root " 0700"
with no default ACL. It holds the resolver watchdog's failure record and nothing
else. The record is 0600 and this directory is 0700 because unlink and rename are
decided by the containing directory and not by a file's own mode, so a 0600 file
in the group\-writable
.B /run/mosdns
above could still be deleted, or replaced with a streak that has already reached
its window, by
.BR mosdns\-router\-cdn .
No other identity in this package reads the record.
.PP
The record is on a tmpfs, so it does not survive a reboot, and a machine that has
just been rebooted gets its window again rather than inheriting one. That tmpfs
is the guard in force.
.PP
The record also names the boot it belongs to, which is a second answer to the same
question for a machine whose
.B /run
is a disk rather than a tmpfs \- and it does
.B not
fire in this package's own unit.
.BR mosdns\-watchdog.service (5)
carries the packaged
.BR ProcSubset=pid ,
which mounts a reduced
.B /proc
with no
.BR /proc/sys ,
so the kernel's
.I boot_id
cannot be read inside it and the record carries no boot id. Each run says so.
Run
.B "mosdns_installer.py watchdog"
by hand, where
.B /proc/sys
is readable, and the boot id is recorded and a carried\-over streak re\-seeded
rather than acted on.
.SH ACCOUNTS AND REMOVAL
A purge removes
.BR /var/lib/mosdns ,
the ownership marker, the NetworkManager dispatcher hook under
.IR /etc/NetworkManager/dispatcher.d/no-wait.d/ ,
and the bridge's
.B __pycache__
directory under
.IR /usr/lib/mosdns-router/mosdns_dhcp_bridge/ .
The last of those is not a package file: Python writes the cache beside the bridge
module the first time anything imports it, so it appears after unpacking, and
.BR dpkg (1)
leaves a directory it does not own with the warning "not empty so not removed".
.PP
A purge also removes the three service accounts
.RB ( mosdns\-router ,
.BR mosdns\-router\-cdn ,
.BR mosdns\-router\-dnscrypt )
and the two groups
.RB ( mosdns\-router ,
.BR mosdns\-router\-dnscrypt ).
Each name carries this package's own, which is the point of them: before, they were
.BR mosdns ,
.BR mosdns\-cdn
and
.BR dnscrypt\-proxy ,
and the third of those is the upstream
.B dnscrypt\-proxy
package's own account. An upgrade from the earlier names moves the four state
directories to the new group and then removes the old accounts; a purge that finds
an account still in use leaves it and says so, rather than taking the name away from
a process that is using it.
.PP
"Still in use" is two checks, and both are reported by name. A process running as
the account, or a file outside this package's own four directories that the account
owns, means something else is using the identity. The account is then
.B left alone
and the message names the command that would remove it
.RB ( deluser\ \-\-system
or
.BR delgroup\ \-\-system ),
because that is a decision for whoever owns the machine rather than one a package
should take for them.
The recorded NetworkManager settings are the one thing a removal cannot recreate.
A plain
.B dpkg \-\-remove
leaves them in place for exactly that reason: a machine with no marker is a
machine a later install will treat as somebody else's. Put them back first with
.BR "mosdns\-cdnctl emergency\-rollback" (1).
.SH OPTIONS
The router takes one subcommand and one flag, and this package's unit uses both:
.B start
and
.BR \-c .
.SH EXIT STATUS
The router is a long\-running server and does not have a useful exit status of its
own; a unit that has failed says why in the journal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR "mosdns\-cdnctl" (1),
.BR dnscrypt\-proxy (8),
